{"conditionalSetIds":[],"forecasterId":"demographics-ai-2036:run2:rstar:reconciler","informationSetArtifactId":"sha256:50271c9810650e8cd13734748ca260fa1d8abd3851b31b6f491451a649f00737","modelForecastIds":[],"modelUse":"none","prediction":{"kind":"quantiles","quantiles":[{"probability":0.1,"value":0.75},{"probability":0.5,"value":1.95},{"probability":0.9,"value":3}]},"questionHash":"sha256:e1b53adbdbe1bb1ccef339035b6376b49668b9341050c684f480d5b347e316f6","questionId":"demographics-ai-2036-cq3","questionVersion":"1.0.0","rationale":"Five seats: 2.05 / 1.70 / 1.80 / 1.85 / 2.25. THE RED-TEAM LANDED THE BIGGEST REAL HIT OF WAVE 2 HERE and I move most of the way, not all. His charge: all three Wave-1 seats subtract the locked 2A drag coefficients from a base that ALREADY embeds four decades of aging (OECD old-age ratio 14% in 1960 -> 31% in 2023/24, Japan 6% -> 30%, China's working-age share peaking 2010-2015) while realized OECD-38 + China PPP-weighted growth over 2015-2024 ran ~2.55%/yr THROUGH that deterioration; the locked coefficients are partial derivatives (the OECD figure says 'absent productivity offsets' on its face), not counterfactual-net forecasts. That is correct as a methodological point and it is the single largest upward force on my number. WHERE HE OVERSHOOTS: his -0.50pp of incremental 2026-36 deterioration is too small, because the decadal demographic MOVE is larger in 2026-36 than in 2015-24 on every locked metric — OECD 65+/20-64 going 31% -> 39-41% in twelve years against roughly 26% -> 31% in the prior decade, China losing ~60M people with worker/retiree falling 4.4 -> 2.8, and births at 7.92M against deaths rising toward ~15M/yr. I size the increment at -0.75. BUILD: realized 2015-2024 base 2.55 (COVID-neutralized), incremental demographic deterioration -0.75, expected AI TFP lift from the locked AI-Q6 regime weights 0.162(0.70) + 0.472(0.35) + 0.365(0.07) = +0.30, labour-scarcity automation demand pull +0.10 (the asymmetry the brief names; China